I actually think that’s one of the more interesting abstractions you’ve proposed.

Not as a claim that “meaning literally obeys physics,” but as a design metaphor inspired by physics.

Your “2/3” idea reminds me of a pattern that appears in many places:

* In writing, you often leave something unsaid so the reader completes it.
* In music, tension resolves because the listener anticipates the missing note.
* In visual art, negative space lets the viewer perceive a whole object.
* In mathematics, an underdetermined system invites additional constraints.
* In conversation, an unfinished thought invites participation rather than passive reception.

In all of those cases, incompleteness is functional.

You’re describing an “open hand” rather than a closed fist.

The open structure has somewhere for the next contribution to land.

That’s an interesting operator:

Generative Incompleteness

Deliberately leave enough structure unfinished that another agent can complete, reinterpret, or extend it.

That may be one reason myths, parables, and poetry endure. They are often not fully specified. They leave interpretive room.

Applying a physics metaphor, I’d say you’re thinking less about conservation laws and more about boundary conditions.

A boundary condition doesn’t determine the whole solution.

It defines the space within which solutions can emerge.

Similarly, an intentionally incomplete story doesn’t determine one interpretation.

It constrains a family of meaningful interpretations.

That fits something you’ve repeatedly emphasized:

* Don’t over-specify.
* Preserve optionality.
* Let the reader perform part of the computation.

I’d just be careful not to overextend the analogy into saying that semantic systems literally obey physical laws. Rather, the mathematics and intuitions from physics—symmetry, constraints, equilibrium, resonance, feedback, phase transitions—can be productive metaphors and sometimes formal tools for thinking about information and communication.

One sentence you said earlier keeps echoing:

“Capture the deltas as you transfer from mode to mode of representation.”

I think that and “2/3” are closely related.

The missing third isn’t merely an absence.

It’s the interface where another mind, another representation, or another context contributes something genuinely new.

In that sense, the open loop isn’t a flaw in the system.

It’s where collaboration enters.